data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** It works just like any other unmanaged type:

```csharp
Span<nint>  span  = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> uspan = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system, `sizeof(nint)` is 8 bytes, so `stackalloc nint[5]` allocates 40 bytes on the stack. The only caveat encountered is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context (since `nint` doesn't have a compile-time-fixed size), but the `stackalloc` itself works fine in safe code when assigned to a `Span<nint>`.
